<!--Post: 4693--><!--Time: 1106683230-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4693"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 25, 2005 - 12:00 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I did a quick search on the &#39;Operation Barn Door&#39; mentioned by our supposed BTK &#40;link to his post below&#41; and found the following information that mentions an &#39;Barn Door&#39; military operation. <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/messages/264/129.html?1105908080" target=_top>http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/messages/264/129.ht ml?1105908080</a> <BR>TO address the connection between myself and op. &#34;Barn door&#34;, I would say that this is false. My duty in the military consisted of radio work which kept my in the field and allowed for several opportunities to kill others. After I was discharged, I went about returning/becoming to/a normal citizen.... <BR>  <BR>EXCERPTED FROM  <BR>LONG ARTTICLE==================================== <BR><a href="http://www.cpcug.org/user/billb/ranchhand/ranchhand.html" target=_top>http://www.cpcug.org/user/billb/ranchhand/ranchhan d.html</a> <BR>  <BR>OPERATION RANCH HAND <BR>HERBICIDES IN SOUTHEAST ASIA <BR>1961-1971 <BR>By <BR>William A. Buckingham, Jr., Ph.D. <BR> <BR>The term &#34;Operation Ranch Hand&#34; was the military code name for spraying herbicides from U.S. Air Force aircraft in Southeast Asia from 1962 through 1971.1 The name itself had no particular significance and was one of a number of similar code names such as &#34;Farm Gate&#34; and&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42;&#42; &#34;Barn Door&#34; &#42;&#42;&#42;that denoted specific military activities early in the Vietnam War.  <BR> <BR> Ranch Hand aircraft were Fairchild C-123s, medium transports with twin piston engines, which later had two jet engines added for extra thrust. The Ranch Hand detachment began with six planes, dropped to two, and peaked at about 25 in 1969. It had several organizational designations over the years, but during the peak spraying years between 1966 and 1970, it was known as the 12th Air Commando Squadron and the 12th Special Operations Squadron. In terms of personnel and aircraft, Ranch Hand was a relatively minor part of overall Air Force operations in Southeast Asia.  <BR> <BR>Between 1962 and 1971, Ranch Hand sprayed about 19 million gallons of herbicide. Eleven million gallons of this total was Agent Orange.2 The spray fell mostly on the forests of South Vietnam, but some was used in Laos, and some killed crops to deprive Viet Cong and North Vietnamese troops of food.  <BR> <BR>E<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4693-->
<!--Post: 4694--><!--Time: 1106683775-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4694"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 25, 2005 - 12:09 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Some other interesting links on this issue: <BR>mention of FACTOR X...not just Vietnam operation BARN DOOR...looks like a connection...??? <BR>E <BR>================================================= <BR>Nat&#39;l Academies Press, Characterizing Exposure of Veterans to ...  - ... program, which resulted Dom the Agent Orange Product Liability ... referencing to the map of Vietnam or by ... Thus, E4 EOI = concentration factor x distance factor x ... <a href="http://www.nap.edu/openbook/NI000510/html/11.html" target=_top>http://www.nap.edu/openbook/NI000510/html/11.html</a>  <BR>[More results from <a href="http://www.nap.edu" target=_top>www.nap.edu</a>]  <BR> <BR>===================================== <BR>BrainTalk Communities - Neurology Support Groups  - ... Factor X deficiency, tiglenz, 0, 12-27-2002 01:35 PM. ... Calling all Viet Nam Veterans with Amyloidosis, gaworden, 0, 10-14-2002 11 ... Amyloidosis and agent orange connection ... <a href="http://neuro-mancer.mgh.harvard.edu/cgi-bin/forumdisplay.cgi?action=topics&amp;forum=Amyloidosis&amp;number=5&amp;DaysPrune=1000&amp;SUBMIT=Go" target=_top>http://neuro-mancer.mgh.harvard.edu/cgi-bin/forumd isplay.cgi?action=topics&amp;forum=Amyloidosis&amp;number= 5&amp;DaysPrune=1000&amp;SUBMIT=Go</a> <BR> <BR> <BR>========================================== <BR>Impact of Agent Orange Exposure among Korean Vietnam Veterans  - ... and 0.39, for brain CT, Chest X-ray, NCV ... Table 3. Behaviroal risk factors of study participants by ... is the assessment of exposure to Agent Orange among Vietnam ... <a href="http://www.niih.go.jp/en/indu_hel/2003/pdf/ih_41_3_03.pdf" target=_top>http://www.niih.go.jp/en/indu_hel/2003/pdf/ih_41_3 _03.pdf</a> <BR> <BR>================================================== == <BR><a href="http://aolsearch.aol.com/aol/search?invocationType=topsearchbox.webhome&amp;query=Factor%2BX%2BAgent%2BOrange%2BVietnam" target=_top>http://aolsearch.aol.com/aol/search?invocationType =topsearchbox.webhome&amp;query=Factor&#43;X&#43;Agent&#43;Orange&#43; Vietnam</a> <BR>Aol search <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4694-->
<!--Post: 4703--><!--Time: 1106689875-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4703"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:hkenley@san.rr.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Chiefron<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Chiefron<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 25, 2005 - 1:51 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->We know that BTK mentioned a factor x. For those that do not know, factor x describes an extremely rare congenital blood clotting disorder. Factor X was discovered in the late 1950s independently in Britain, Sweden and the U.S. &#40;it is commonly called the Stuart Prower Factor after the 2 people it was discovered in, in the U.S. and Britain&#41;. This condition is commonly found in areas where there is high levels of inbreeding. In the U.S. it is between 1 and 500,000 and 1 and 1,000,000. I can find nowhere that Agent Orange itself has been connected to developing Factor X except indirectly via amylodosis. Even then it was not until 1981 that we really started finding concrete links between agent orange and other disorders suffered by Vietnam veterans and it received official recognition as a causitive agent. Factor X can be caused by an insecticide used extensively in Vietname however, methylbromide. That connection was made earlier however and BTK could have been exposed to that in any agricultural area of the U.S. especially living in an area like Wichita surrounding by lots of grain production. <BR> <BR>What it boils down to is the poster is a crank who relates BTK&#39;s factor x, to Vietnam when the medical world would not make that connection for another 4 years. It also ignores the fact that BTK attributes the killing of other famous SKs to factor x. I find it highly unlikely that what basically boils down to being a hemophiliac is the contributing factor to the killings of any of these men. <BR> <BR>Though if BTK did in fact have Stuart Prower Factor it would make him extremely easy to find in Wichita. I would think the list of long term, white or hispanic male plasma infusion patients, born between 1939 and 1951 with diagnosed SPF, would be a relatively short one. <BR> <BR>Ron<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4703-->
<!--Post: 4707--><!--Time: 1106690875-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4707"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 25, 2005 - 2:07 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The Btk gene is located in the X chromosome <BR>&#40;word for word...see below..&#41; <BR> <BR><a href="http://bioinf.uta.fi/BTKbase/intro.html" target=_top>http://bioinf.uta.fi/BTKbase/intro.html</a> <BR>BTKbase: Mutation registry for X-linked agammaglobulinemia <BR> <BR>BTKbase is created by Mauno Vihinen and C.I. Edvard Smith <BR>The database is updated by Jouni Väliaho <BR> <BR>Genomic organization and mutation analysis <BR> <BR>The Btk gene is located in the X chromosome, in the Xq22 region. The human Btk gene encompasses 37.5 kb and contains 19 exons, 18 of which code for the protein. DNA based mutation scanning techniques, such as single­strand conformation polymorphism &#40;SSCP&#41;, identifying also e.g. splice site mutations, are mainly used. As in most other genes, mutations are mainly found in exons and splice sites. Single base pair alterations predominate, and CpG sites are hot spots for mutations. The Btk kinase consists of five structural domain: PH &#40;pleckstrin homology&#41;, TH &#40;Tec homology&#41;, SH3 &#40;Src homology 3&#41;, SH2 and kinase domains. &#40;picture of domains&#41;  <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4707-->
<!--Post: 4712--><!--Time: 1106691042-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4712"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 25, 2005 - 2:10 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_packages/btk/archi" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/news/special_pack ages/btk/archi</a> ve/8348962.htm <BR> <BR>EXCERPTED from original article: <BR> <BR>In excerpts released by police and the television station, the killer said that he was compelled to kill by &#34;factor-X,&#34; the same factor that motivated Son of Sam in New York, Jack the Ripper in London and the Hillside Strangler in Los Angeles. <BR> <BR>======================================= <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4712-->
<!--Post: 4714--><!--Time: 1106691316-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4714"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 25, 2005 - 2:15 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I agree with you Ron.  I posted these links because I find it a rare coincidence that there is actually a BTK gene and a factor X involved in a disease.  However, it is probably just that..A COINCIDENCE.   <BR> <BR>From what I understand, FACTOR X can refer to a common blood co-factor..meaning in this case, FACTOR X in relation to blood disease is just the FACTOR referred to in speaking the language of blood in general.  <BR> <BR>Thanks for your input here.  Makes a lot sense to me.   <BR> <BR>E<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4714-->
<!--Post: 4787--><!--Time: 1106750596-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4787"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Maxgravis<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Maxgravis<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 26, 2005 - 6:43 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I think Factor X is just a referrence to the need to kill, the voices in the head, the demons inside etc. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4787-->
<!--Post: 4789--><!--Time: 1106752357-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4789"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Featherw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Featherw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 26, 2005 - 7:12 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Truthfully, I think you are right Max.  That is probably all it amounts to.  Kind of interesting to see what else it COULD mean...but that is probably it in a nutshell. <BR> <BR>Factor X, if I remember correctly is the factor in the X-men and all the X characters of comics that gives the character the will, ability and control they have.  Either to kill or to save. <BR> <BR>There is actually a comic series called X Factor.  <BR>Thanks.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4789-->
<!--Post: 5764--><!--Time: 1107359794-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5764"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilymarie<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilymarie<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, February 02, 2005 - 7:56 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Feather - thanks for the Factor X and comic series tie-in. Search &#34;crime comics&#34; &#40;and I didn&#39;t know this&#41; the crime comics from the 40&#39;s, 50&#39;s, until they were banned were unbelievable! Lots of domination, objectification, and power/control over females was a running theme. Very sexual and sadistic in nature. One could see how a young/adolescent boy, in the wrong home circumstances, could twist these themes into fantasies. The art is very graphic showing the fear and horror on the women&#39;s faces.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5764-->
<!--Post: 11735--><!--Time: 1111098933-->
